WEBVTT

00:00:00.676 --> 00:00:03.321
Clauses and complete sentences.

00:00:03.321 --> 00:00:06.571
Every sentence needs a subject and a main verb.

00:00:06.571 --> 00:00:09.958
Get this, and half the punctuation questions get easier.

00:00:11.536 --> 00:00:13.431
Here's how to spot one.

00:00:13.431 --> 00:00:19.666
The choices use the same words, with different punctuation, or a verb in different forms.

00:00:19.666 --> 00:00:23.017
And the question asks about the conventions of Standard English.

00:00:24.081 --> 00:00:28.439
It's testing one thing: where a complete sentence starts, and where it stops.

00:00:29.481 --> 00:00:30.961
Here's the method.

00:00:30.961 --> 00:00:33.171
First, find the subject.

00:00:33.171 --> 00:00:35.056
Who or what is this about?

00:00:35.056 --> 00:00:37.086
Then find its main verb.

00:00:37.086 --> 00:00:40.766
Words like stretching, or to stretch, don't count.

00:00:40.766 --> 00:00:42.951
Now test each side of the blank.

00:00:42.951 --> 00:00:45.491
Could it stand alone as a sentence?

00:00:45.491 --> 00:00:53.422
If both sides are full sentences, they need a real break: a period, a semicolon, or a comma with a FANBOYS word.

00:00:54.531 --> 00:00:58.401
Rule one: a sentence needs a subject and a main verb.

00:00:58.401 --> 00:01:00.846
The storm ending isn't a sentence.

00:01:00.846 --> 00:01:02.936
Ending can't be a main verb.

00:01:02.936 --> 00:01:04.045
The storm ended is.

00:01:05.106 --> 00:01:10.716
Rule two: words like because, although, and which make a clause incomplete.

00:01:10.716 --> 00:01:14.301
Because it rained, on its own, is a fragment.

00:01:14.301 --> 00:01:16.173
It needs a full sentence attached.

00:01:17.243 --> 00:01:21.813
Rule three: a comma alone can never join two full sentences.

00:01:21.813 --> 00:01:23.878
That's called a comma splice.

00:01:23.878 --> 00:01:28.521
Use a period, a semicolon, or a comma plus a FANBOYS word.

00:01:29.585 --> 00:01:31.060
Let's try one.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:34.115
The subject is the Atacama Desert in Chile.

00:01:34.115 --> 00:01:37.615
The part between the commas is just extra description.

00:01:37.615 --> 00:01:39.980
So, where's the main verb?

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:43.290
There isn't one yet, so the blank has to supply it.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:45.145
Now look at the choices.

00:01:45.145 --> 00:01:49.790
Receiving, having received, and to receive can't be main verbs.

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:54.200
Receives is a real main verb, and it makes the sentence complete.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:55.205
The answer is A.

00:01:56.273 --> 00:01:58.548
Watch out for the long fragment.

00:01:58.548 --> 00:02:02.608
A sentence can go on and on, and still have no main verb.

00:02:02.608 --> 00:02:05.063
This one never says what Petra does.

00:02:05.063 --> 00:02:07.245
Add was, and now it's complete.

00:02:08.281 --> 00:02:14.941
Here's the whole lesson on one page: how to spot it, the four steps, the rules, and the trap.

00:02:14.941 --> 00:02:17.761
Pause the video now, and copy it into your notes.
